welcome everyone from  around the world thank you for joining  us for today's presentation  entitled cancer transcriptome atlas with  ngs readout the essential tool for  spatial genomics  in oncology and it's my real pleasure to  introduce you to kit  furman he's the geomics rna product  manager at nanostring technologies  and for a complete biography of kit and  all our speakers you can visit the tab  at the bottom of your screen now if you  have any questions during the  presentation  please submit them in the q a box below  and our speaker will respond  back to you via email so please join me  in welcoming kit  and i'll turn the presentation over to  him  hello thank you for joining my name is  kip vernon i am the product manager for  the geomex rna  assays today i would like to introduce  our cancer transcriptome atlas  which is an essential tool for spatial  genomics and oncology research  so genomics research has evolved quite  considerably over the last 15 years  bulk gene expression has taught us much  about tumor biology  just like that fruit smoothie on the  left we've had to homogenize the tissue  for this to work  even so these gene expression signatures  has taught us a lot  about the state of the tumor about how  it's inflamed  um and how can we we can inform  different  uh decisions so the next iteration  was single cell biology where we're able  to pick apart and figure out  all the different pieces of fruit that  went into the fruit smoothing  we can discern different cell types and  different phenotypes and functionality  in these cells but putting it back  together  is where we struggle right now putting  it back in the original framework and  the original architecture of the tissue  is a very difficult task this is where  the power of the digital spatial  profiler  comes into play it allows the user to  see the spatial landscape  and quantify expression across important  regions of interest  today we are introducing the cancer  transcriptomatilis or what we call the  cta  combined with the ngs readout for the  geomets dsp platform  the cta is the first genomics assay to  leverage the almost unlimited plex of  our new ngs readout chemistry  we've built reagent probes sets that  allow  an almost limited number of targets to  be quantified simultaneously  with the cta you can measure 1834  different mrna gene transcripts  throughout the tumor  immune system and the micro environment  importantly our technology is not  limited by high expressos  dsp enriches for transcripts that are  critical to your research  regardless of expression levels and you  can  map these gene expression values  directly  to regions within your tissue  so when designing this probe set we  began by adding in all the important  immune targets  the immune system plays a critical role  in oncology and in many other diseases  so we included genes involved in innate  and adaptive immune systems  any researcher studying the immune  system whether infectious disease  or oncology will find these targets  informative  we also layered in targets from our key  encounter panels including the pan  cancer pathways and io 360 panels  we added clinically relevant gene sets  um and we added in uh metabolic genes  um there are considerable number of  metabolic gene targets in this panel  and it allows the researcher to study  the metabolic state of the  tumor and the immune system  importantly we also added in a lot of  information and gene targets through  community  input we mine both the literature  and other presentations and online gene  sets in databases  to make sure that all the important  informative targets were in this panel  finally we fully annotated the probe set  this allows the user to bring context to  every gene  in this atlas the cta includes genes to  identify critical immune cell types such  as t cells and macrophage subsets  we've also added gene sets that offer  insight into immune biology such as the  tumor information set  and the pam 50 gene signatures  the cancer transcript atlas encompasses  over 95  different biological pathways and has  many annotations built in  having all this great content in one  place is made possible by our unique  chemistry  our probes hybridize directly to  transcripts within  the formally fixed parameter tissue  section or your fresh frozen tissue  section  the readout portion which we call the  dsp barcode  contains a readout tag sequence for  every target and  a unique molecular identifier for every  molecule  we also included the truseq primary  regions to make the readout compatible  with an illumina sequencer  the photocleavable linker between the  probe and the readout portion is a key  part of the dsp platform  we use uv light to collect the dsp  barcode  for downstream analysis for specific  regions of interest  any project begins with staining a  tissue section with the cancer  transcriptome atlas probes  and a set of up to four morphological  markers including a dna stape  this is step one the morphology markers  can either be  antibodies with fluorescent labels or  rna scope probes to visualize individual  transcripts  the markers allow us to visualize the  tissue sections  and guides the region of interest or roi  selections  this is step two  so i would like to take a moment just to  talk about the rna scope probes that  we've integrated into  the geometry flow visualizing and  understanding the landscape of favorite  tissue is critical to connecting the  genomics information  to spatial context rna scope is  integrated is an integrated tool  to visualize single transcripts  you can visualize the three different  transcripts and you can choose from our  list of predefined rna scope  virtual kits or you can build your own  combination  rna scope is a unique tool for selecting  regions and often times  difficult to target gene targets such as  tgf beta and box b3  so i'd like to give you an example of  how we go about selecting regions of  interest  here we have non-small cell lung  carcinoma tissue section  and it's been stained with four  morphological markers  dna cd3  pdcd1 which is the transcript for pd-1  and cd-274 which is the transcript for  pdl1  so these are immune checkpoint genes and  so we've  scanned the whole tissue section and  then we've zoomed in  and selected individual regions of  interest  we've used our morphology stain to  select three different region types  across the whole tissue so multiple  regions of the same type  um on the top you can see that we've  selected regions that are high for pdc1  and the stroma  and then regions below which are in the  tumor which are  low for pd1 and then on the right we've  selected tumor regions that are high for  pd1 so  in this way we can categorize the  different regions  um that we're selecting  dsp importantly allows us to study the  transcriptional  profile of each of these regions  independently  so now that we've stained our tissue and  selected the regions of interest  the machine will then illuminate every  single  region it will the barcodes and dsp  barcodes  will come off of the tissue they will be  collected by the instrument  and deposited in a well in a 96 well  collection plate this process is then  repeated for steps three and four for  every single  uh region that the user has selected at  the  at this point the clutch plate is then  processed in our library prep  and sequenced on the luminous sequencer  now that each region is one well in our  collection plate  we can take it and process the dsb  barcodes  nanostring provides all the reagents you  need to  perform the library prep then once it's  sequenced  we provide all the tools needed to take  the data  from fast q files to analyzing the data  to construct the library prep management  has developed the geometry c  code reagent to process and collect the  dsp barcode logos  from the cta assay we've built an eight  plate set of  universal dual index adapters these  indices are properly color balanced  indices so that you can process these  barcodes in the order they are collected  no need to cherry pick wells  then the system will will actually  identify which  how to set up the library prep we use  the dual indices to map the dc  barcodes from the illuminated regions to  the resulting genomic cta gene  expression data  we also provide the pcr enzymes with the  seek code  along with the well specific primers the  pcr has the p5 and p7 regions  to the library constructs once the  library is generated you can pull all of  the wells together and run them on an  aluminum sequencer  you can multiplex a minimum of four  wells or all  768 wells from all eight unique plates  the seco library prep are compatible  across a wide range of illumina  sequencers  the number of reads needed depends on  the size of the region selected  for a typical region that is between two  to three million reads  for each slide with the typical number  of regions between 12 or 24 regions  you'd want to plan for 30 to 50 million  reads  only single end reads are needed but you  could certainly run the library with  paired and reads if you'd like  once the library has been sequenced  manage string provides the tools to  process the fast q files and transform  them into digital counts  you take the vast q files from your  lumia sequencer data run  and then we have an automated data  processing pipeline that then converts  them into these dcc files you then will  upload these dcc files into the genomics  platform and it will connect  the gene expression data back to each  specific region of interest  you can now build your analysis in dsp  in the dsp data analysis suite  on the left hand side we have the slide  explorer we can add in all the slides  from our study  this is a critical part of the platform  where you can  build these data analyses from multiple  sections and multiple slides  so you can build an analysis across  large cohorts of samples  from clinical trials data sets to  large internal studies  the software has all the tools to check  the quality of your data  normalize your reads and analyze the  data for interpretation  the middle section keeps track of any  changes you make to the data  and has a list of all the gene  annotations that we talked about  you can also visualize your data in  interesting and informative ways  importantly the software is interactive  and the genetic data is always tracked  back to the region  and of interest of the segment that  you're working with  so going back to our non-smell cell  along carcinoma samples  here we have these two region of  interest types  pl1 high and low regions in the tumor  the dsp data analysis we can analyze all  the similar regions and then compare the  expression  profiles between the two region types so  here we're looking at on the right is a  volcano plot  comparing the cd274 high and the two  cd 274 low regions  and you can get a list of all the  differentially expressed  genes between those two region types  this is just one  sample but you can expand this analysis  across  a large data set of samples  i would also encourage you to check out  our colorectal carcinoma whitening paper  that uses the cancer transcriptome atlas  to study the differences between um  two different main types of crc sample  types  one which is mss and one which is msi  these two different  clinical annotations have very different  outcomes and we use  three different profiling types to  analyze  these this this set of samples  we choose different region of interest  selection methods  um across multiple samples um  tantalize the immune system and how it's  changing both  across the individual samples and  between these  two different very different sample  types  so in review the cancer transcript  analyst  has an expansive collection of content  over 1800 genes across  95 annotated pathways it is very  comprehensive  um and includes targets in tumor biology  immune response and the microenvironment  it has critical gene signatures and you  can actually add in  targets of interest it has a minimal  sequencing requirement between 30 and 15  million reads per sample you can try it  now  in our technology access program and  it's going to ship  in august 2020. thank you for  joining this presentation  [Music]  thanks kid that's a really exciting new  product um wonderful presentation we  have  a number of questions for you now so um  the first is that  i work often with frozen tissue sections  can samples preserved in  oct be analyzed with the cta  you can use fresh frozen samples samples  that have been processed oct  on the gmx dsp platform we have a  protocol  for you to process your oct or fresh  frozen samples  to run on the genomics rna assay and the  cta  the second question relates to the  application of cta  to transplant biology how might that  the cancer transcriptome atlas be  applicable to other settings like that  we actually have quite a large number of  immune targets in the cancer  transcription atlas  these targets are spread across both  adaptive  and innate immunology and would be  applicable to any researcher who's doing  immunology related research  uh here's a third question for you kit i  work with very specific protease genes  for my research can i add them to the  cta  absolutely customers can add targets  on top of the cta for trans genes  or for genes that are not targeted in  the cta  that you can add up to 30 targets  to cta  okay we have another question here which  is my ngs core lab is in another  building how soon after i collect the  dsp barcodes  should i run the library prep  you can store the collection plate at  negative 20 for as long as you need to  and then you can thaw it and process the  library prep on  yourself or you can send it to your geo  genomics core  to do the library processing  and your final question kit which is i  just generated a single cell data set  with my samples can geomic spatial  profiling help me analyze my other data  sets  well that's a great question we're sure  that you're going to have other genomics  data sets that you're going to want to  integrate  into cta data i would actually encourage  you to check out a poster here in this  virtual  college conference from patrick danahar  that actually answers  this question  so i think we'd uh we'd all agree that  we've had a series of really wonderful  presentations today really exciting  science  and i want to um acknowledge and thank  all our speakers for participating  and sharing this really exciting data  with us  i think this is a very exciting and fast  moving field  the new technologies that are emerging  are i think you'll all agree  enabling us to answer questions that  were previously just intractable  and so i really look forward to some  wonderful new discoveries in this space  i want to also thank you for attending  and for making this conference  i want to acknowledge nanastring for  putting together this wonderful meeting  and i hope you can all tune in tomorrow  for the second day  in this virtual oncology conference  thank you

